<h2>Fractions Round Table</h2>
<p><a href="https://im.kendallhunt.com/k5/teachers/grade-3/unit-8/lesson-3/lesson.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Original 2021 source round-table statement illustrations (teacher review required)</a>. The source images, original student PDF, and publisher-gated Cool-down are not copied or visually certified here.</p>
<p>Start by modelling <strong>1/8</strong> using equal parts of one whole and a number line. Then work in groups of four: discuss the fraction statements that the teacher selects from the source table, about parts, wholes, values, comparisons and lengths.</p>
<ol><li><strong>Round 1:</strong> each learner chooses agree, disagree, or unsure and explains a reason.</li>
<li><strong>Round 2:</strong> each learner responds to others' reasoning, with an opportunity to change views.</li>
<li><strong>Round 3:</strong> after discussion, each learner records a final stance and explains any revision.</li></ol>
<p>Independent checks: 5/4 is greater than 1; whole numbers can be written n/1; not all fractions are whole numbers. 1/2 is numerically greater than 1/3 with the same unit; portions of different-size wholes need extra context. These teacher-authored examples do not replace the source illustrations or dialogue.</p>
<p><strong>Teacher rubric:</strong> record participation, proper equal-parts representation, fraction vocabulary, evidence for positions and thoughtful justified revisions. Do not auto-grade an open discussion solely by a fixed A/B/C selection.</p>
</section>
